  "documentation": {
    "range": "<p><em>Synanceia verrucosa</em>&#160;is distributed throughout the Indo-West Pacific from the Red Sea and the east coast of Africa to Sri Lanka, the South China Sea, Japan and south to South Australia and French Polynesia (Grobecker 1983, Poss and Rama Rao 1984, Randall and Lim 2000). A single specimen has been recorded off the coast of Turkey (Bilecenoglu 2012) and several museum records show it to be distributed at the Marshall Islands (accessed via FishNet2 on 07/06/2017). This species occurs to 20 m depth (Bilecenoglu 2012).&#160;</p>",
    "population": "<p>Information relating to the population of this species is limited, however, there are over 200 records for this species held in museum collections around the world. Most records are for only single specimens (accessed via FishNet2 on 07/06/2017), indicating that this species is likely not abundant throughout its range.</p>",
    "habitats": "<em>Synanceia verrucosa </em>is a marine species found in shallow waters among coral reefs and coral rubble and in pools at low tide levels. It is an extremely venomous species. It is known to grow to a maximum standard length of 40 cm but usually only to 20 cm (Poss and Rama Rao 1984).",
    "threats": "There are no major threats known to impact this species at this time.",
    "measures": "There are no species-specific conservation measures currently in place, though parts of its range do overlap with marine protected areas (IUCN-UNEP 2017).",
    "use_trade": "This species is incidentally caught throughout its range but is not known to be targeted and is uncommon in markets (Poss and Rama Rao 1984).",
    "rationale": "<p><em>Synanceia verrucosa</em> is a highly venomous, marine species that is distributed throughout the Indo-Pacific. Population information for this species is limited, though it is well represented in museum collections. It is caught incidentally throughout its range, though apparently not targeted and it is not known to be impacted by pervasive threats. There are no species-specific conservation measures in place for it, however, its range overlaps with many existing marine protected areas. This species is therefore assessed as Least Concern.</p>",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
